    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/30-1-rituals",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Rituals",
      "slug": "30-1-rituals",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Rituals",
        "section": "30.1",
        "heading": "Rituals",
        "lineStart": 2415,
        "lineEnd": 2417
      },
      "section": "30.1",
      "rulesText": "To enact a ritual is to come into contact with these abyssal forces and entreat them to do your will. It is a practice not without considerable risk."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/30-2-finding-a-ritual-source",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Finding a ritual source",
      "slug": "30-2-finding-a-ritual-source",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Rituals",
        "section": "30.2",
        "heading": "Finding a ritual source",
        "lineStart": 2419,
        "lineEnd": 2421
      },
      "section": "30.2",
      "rulesText": "A PC with the **Ritual** special ability begins with one known ritual, already learned (answer the questions below to create it). To learn a new ritual, a PC must first find a **source**. A source may be secured as payoff from a score—perhaps you steal a ritual book when your crew robs the Museum of Antiquities. You might also secure a source as the goal of a long-term project—by consorting with cultist friends, studying ancient texts, or some other method you devise."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/30-3-learning-a-ritual",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Learning a ritual",
      "slug": "30-3-learning-a-ritual",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Rituals",
        "section": "30.3",
        "heading": "Learning a ritual",
        "lineStart": 2423,
        "lineEnd": 2425
      },
      "section": "30.3",
      "rulesText": "Once the source of a ritual is found, you may undertake a long-term project to learn the ritual. Most rituals will require an 8-segment progress clock to learn. The player and the GM answer questions about the ritual to define what it will do in play and what is required to perform it (see below). The player records these answers in their notes for future reference."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/30-4-ritual-questions",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Ritual Questions",
      "slug": "30-4-ritual-questions",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Rituals",
        "section": "30.4",
        "heading": "Ritual Questions",
        "lineStart": 2427,
        "lineEnd": 2431
      },
      "section": "30.4",
      "rulesText": "1. GM asks: **\"What does the ritual do and how is it weird?\"** Player answers.\n2. Player asks: **\"What must I do to perform the ritual, and what is its price?\"** GM answers. A ritual takes at least one downtime activity to perform and inflicts **stress** on the caster according to its **magnitude**.  If performance of the ritual is dangerous or troublesome in some way, it requires an action roll (usually **Attune**). A ritual may also have additional costs, such as a sacrifice, rare item, the start of a dire progress clock, etc.\n3. GM asks: **\"What new belief or fear does knowledge of this ritual and its attendant occult forces instill in you?\"** Player answers."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/30-5-example-ritual-answers",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Example Ritual Answers",
      "slug": "30-5-example-ritual-answers",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Rituals",
        "section": "30.5",
        "heading": "Example Ritual Answers",
        "lineStart": 2433,
        "lineEnd": 2439
      },
      "section": "30.5",
      "rulesText": "> Player: \"The ritual wards a person so that the ghosts of their victims cannot find them. It's weird because... as long as the ward is in place, the person sometimes weeps tears of black blood.\"\n>\n> GM: \"Spend a downtime action to prepare a mixture of tobacco, dream smoke, and crematory ash from a victim—which the target then smokes. You take at least 3 stress when you perform the ritual, which will be its quality for a fortune roll when it's challenged by a spirit—so you might want to take more stress to make it more potent.\"\n>\n> Player: \"Gotcha. My new fear is what will happen if the spirits figure out where the ward came from and turn their vengeance on me, instead.\""
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/30-6-performing-a-ritual",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Performing a Ritual",
      "slug": "30-6-performing-a-ritual",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Rituals",
        "section": "30.6",
        "heading": "Performing a Ritual",
        "lineStart": 2441,
        "lineEnd": 2453
      },
      "section": "30.6",
      "rulesText": "To perform a ritual, you must have the **Ritual** special ability, then follow the method outlined by the answers to the ritual questions. Most rituals will take **one downtime activity** to complete, though the GM may call for two (or more) downtime activities for very powerful or far-reaching rituals. Some rituals may be partially performed during downtime and then fully manifested at-will later by completing the last incantation or ritual action. In this case, simply make a note that the ritual has been \"primed\" and may be unleashed at a later time.\n\nWhen you perform a ritual, you take an amount of **stress** as established by the ritual questions, according to the **magnitude** of the forces brought to bear. The GM uses magnitude as a *guideline* for setting the stress cost—it may be higher or lower at their discretion to better describe the nature of the ritual. Some claims and special abilities also reduce the stress cost for ritual casting (like the Cult's **Ancient Obelisk** claim).\n\n**Rituals take time to cast.** Use the duration examples on the magnitude table to reduce the stress cost based on the time needed, generally no less than an hour.\n\nThe GM may also **tick a progress clock** when you perform a ritual—to advance the agenda of an arcane power or entity, or to show the steady approach of a dark outcome that is a consequence of the ritual's use.\n\nIf a ritual is dangerous or troublesome to perform, make an **action roll** (usually **Attune**) to see if unpleasant consequences manifest. If a ritual has an uncertain effect then a **fortune roll** should be made to see how well it manifests. Because a ritual is a downtime activity, you may spend **coin** 1-for-1 to increase the result level of your fortune roll (this represents the expenditure of expensive or rare ritual materials). If a ritual is both dangerous and uncertain, then both rolls may be called for.\n\nEach performance of a ritual is a unique event, and may not always work the same way each time. The GM or players may call for a round of questions to establish a ritual anew. Rituals are a way to bring in a wide variety of arcane effects into the game. Use with caution! If you ever go overboard, address the questions again to establish new weirdness and costs if things have gotten out of hand. The abyssal forces are not playthings and cannot be considered a reliable or safe source of power."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-1-crafting",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Crafting",
      "slug": "31-1-crafting",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.1",
        "heading": "Crafting",
        "lineStart": 2455,
        "lineEnd": 2457
      },
      "section": "31.1",
      "rulesText": "During downtime, a PC can **Tinker** with special materials and tools to produce strange **alchemicals**, **build** (or **modify**) **items**, **create spark-craft** gadgets, or enchant **arcane** implements or weapons. The system for each method is similar, with different details depending on the nature of the project."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-2-inventing",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Inventing",
      "slug": "31-2-inventing",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.2",
        "heading": "Inventing",
        "lineStart": 2459,
        "lineEnd": 2461
      },
      "section": "31.2",
      "rulesText": "To invent a formula for a new alchemical concoction or the plan for a new item of your design, you need to **Study** it as a **long-term project**. Most new formulas or designs will require an 8-segment progress clock to invent and learn. The player and the GM answer questions about the invention to define what it will do in play and what is required to create it (see below). The player records these answers in their notes for future reference."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-3-creation-questions",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Creation Questions",
      "slug": "31-3-creation-questions",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.3",
        "heading": "Creation Questions",
        "lineStart": 2463,
        "lineEnd": 2474
      },
      "section": "31.3",
      "rulesText": "1. GM asks: **\"What type of creation is it and what does it do?\"** Player answers. A creation might be mundane, alchemical, arcane, or spark-craft. If a PC has an appropriate special ability (**Alchemist**, **Artificer**, **Strange Methods**), they get bonuses when inventing and crafting certain creation types.\n2. Player asks: **\"What's the minimum quality level of this item?\"** GM answers, with the **magnitude** of the effects the item produces as a guideline.\n3. GM asks: **\"What rare, strange, or adverse aspect of this formula or design has kept it in obscurity, out of common usage?**\" Player answers.\n4. Player asks: **\"What drawbacks does this item have, if any?\"** GM answers by choosing one or more from the drawbacks list, or by saying there are none.\n\nA PC with the **Alchemist**, **Artificer**, or **Strange Methods** special abilities invents and learns one special formula when they take the ability (they don't have to take time to learn it).\n\nOnce you've invented a formula or design, you can **craft** it by using a downtime activity (see **Crafting**, below). No one else can craft this invention unless they learn your design as a long-term project. If you acquire a formula or design invented by another tinkerer, you may learn to craft it by completing a long-term project.\n\nCommon alchemicals (see **Sample Creations** for more information) and ordinary items don't require special formulas or designs to learn. Anyone may attempt to craft them by using commonly available instructions."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-4-crafting",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Crafting",
      "slug": "31-4-crafting",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.4",
        "heading": "Crafting",
        "lineStart": 2476,
        "lineEnd": 2482
      },
      "section": "31.4",
      "rulesText": "To craft something, spend one **downtime activity** to make a **Tinker** roll to determine the **quality level** of the item you produce. The base quality level is equal to your crew's Tier, modified by the result of the roll (see the results in the next section).\n\nThe results are based on your crew's Tier because it indicates the overall quality of the workspace and materials you have access to. *If you do the work with the **Workshop** upgrade for your crew, your effective Tier level is one higher for this roll.*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-5-crafting-roll",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "CRAFTING ROLL",
      "slug": "31-5-crafting-roll",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.5",
        "heading": "CRAFTING ROLL",
        "lineStart": 2484,
        "lineEnd": 2501
      },
      "section": "31.5",
      "rulesText": "&#x23E3; &nbsp; **1d** for each **Tinker** action dot.\n\n&#x2295; &nbsp; **+1** quality per **Coin** spent.\n\n&#x2295; &nbsp; **+1** quality for **Workshop** crew upgrade.\n\n* **Critical**: Quality level is Tier +2.\n* **6**: Quality level is Tier +1.\n* **4/5**: Quality level is equal to Tier.\n* **1-3**: Quality level is Tier -1.\n\n\nThe GM sets a **minimum quality** level that must be achieved to craft the item, based on the **magnitude** of the effect(s) it produces. The GM uses magnitude as a *guideline* for setting the quality level—it may be higher or lower at their discretion to better describe the nature of the project. An item may be crafted at *higher* quality if the player wishes to attempt it.\n\nYou may spend **coin** 1-for-1 to increase the final quality level result of your roll (this can raise quality level beyond Tier +2)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-6-modifying-an-item",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Modifying an item",
      "slug": "31-6-modifying-an-item",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.6",
        "heading": "Modifying an item",
        "lineStart": 2503,
        "lineEnd": 2511
      },
      "section": "31.6",
      "rulesText": "Adding a feature or additional function to an item is simpler than creating something new. You don't need to invent a special formula or plan. Make a crafting roll to modify an item (the baseline quality of an item that you modify is equal to your crew's Tier, as usual).\n\n* A simple, useful modification requires Tier +1 quality. *A rifle that breaks down into two sections to be more easily concealed.*\n* A significant modification requires Tier +2 quality. *Strengthening the barrel and powder load of a gun to fire further.*\n* An arcane, spark-craft, or alchemical modification requires Tier +3 quality. *A dagger that can harm a demon. An electrified hull on a boat to repel boarders or ghosts. An outfit coated with chemicals to mask you from deathlands predators.*\n\nModified items, like special creations, may have **drawbacks**."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/31-7-drawbacks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Drawbacks",
      "slug": "31-7-drawbacks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Crafting",
        "section": "31.7",
        "heading": "Drawbacks",
        "lineStart": 2513,
        "lineEnd": 2522
      },
      "section": "31.7",
      "rulesText": "A creation or modification may have one or more drawbacks, chosen by the GM.\n\n* **Complex**. You'll have to create it in multiple stages; the GM will tell you how many. One downtime activity and crafting roll is needed per stage.\n* **Conspicuous**. This creation doesn't go unnoticed. Take **+1** **heat** if it's used any number of times on an operation.\n* **Consumable**. This creation has a limited number of uses (all alchemicals must have this drawback, usually one use).\n* **Rare**. This creation requires a rare item or material when it is crafted.\n* **Unreliable**. When you use the item, make a fortune roll (using its **quality**) to see how well it performs.\n* **Volatile**. The item produces a dangerous or troublesome side-effect for the user, specified by the GM. A side-effect is a consequence, and may be **resisted**."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/4-1-stress-trauma",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Stress & Trauma",
      "slug": "4-1-stress-trauma",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Stress & Trauma",
        "section": "4.1",
        "heading": "Stress & Trauma",
        "lineStart": 210,
        "lineEnd": 210
      },
      "section": "4.1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/4-2-stress",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Stress",
      "slug": "4-2-stress",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Stress & Trauma",
        "section": "4.2",
        "heading": "Stress",
        "lineStart": 212,
        "lineEnd": 218
      },
      "section": "4.2",
      "rulesText": "Player characters in *Blades in the Dark* have a special reserve called **stress**. When they suffer a consequence that they don't want to accept, they can take stress instead. The result of the **resistance roll** determines how much stress it costs to avoid a bad outcome.\n\n*During a knife fight, Daniel's character, Cross, gets stabbed in the chest. Daniel rolls his **Prowess** rating to resist, and gets a **2**. It costs 6 stress, minus 2 (the result of the resistance roll) to resist the consequences. Daniel marks off 4 stress and describes how Cross survives.*\n\n*The GM rules that the harm is reduced by the resistance roll, but not avoided entirely. Cross suffers level 2 harm (\"Chest Wound\") instead of level 3 harm (\"Punctured Lung\").*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/4-3-pushing-yourself",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Pushing Yourself",
      "slug": "4-3-pushing-yourself",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Stress & Trauma",
        "section": "4.3",
        "heading": "Pushing Yourself",
        "lineStart": 220,
        "lineEnd": 226
      },
      "section": "4.3",
      "rulesText": "You can use stress to push yourself for greater performance. For each bonus you choose below, take **2 stress** (each can be chosen once for a given action):\n\n* Add **+1d** to your roll. (This may be used for an action roll or downtime roll or any other kind of roll where extra effort would help you)\n* Add **+1 level** to your effect.\n* Take action when you're incapacitated."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/4-4-trauma",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Trauma",
      "slug": "4-4-trauma",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Stress & Trauma",
        "section": "4.4",
        "heading": "Trauma",
        "lineStart": 228,
        "lineEnd": 234
      },
      "section": "4.4",
      "rulesText": "When a PC marks their last stress box, they suffer a level of **trauma**. When you take **trauma**, circle one of your **trauma conditions** like *Cold*, *Reckless*, *Unstable*, etc. They're all described in the next section.\n\nWhen you suffer **trauma**, you're taken out of action. You're \"left for dead\" or otherwise dropped out of the current conflict, only to come back later, shaken and drained. When you return, **you have zero stress** and your vice has been satisfied for the next downtime.\n\n**Trauma conditions are permanent**. Your character acquires the new personality quirk indicated by the condition, and can earn xp by using it to cause trouble. **When you mark your fourth trauma condition**, your character cannot continue as a daring scoundrel. You must retire them to a different life or send them to prison to take the fall for the crew's **wanted level**."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/4-5-trauma-conditions",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Trauma Conditions",
      "slug": "4-5-trauma-conditions",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Stress & Trauma",
        "section": "4.5",
        "heading": "Trauma Conditions",
        "lineStart": 236,
        "lineEnd": 245
      },
      "section": "4.5",
      "rulesText": "* **Cold**: You're not moved by emotional appeals or social bonds.\n* **Haunted**: You're often lost in reverie, reliving past horrors, seeing things.\n* **Obsessed**: You're enthralled by one thing: an activity, a person, an ideology.\n* **Paranoid**: You imagine danger everywhere; you can't trust others.\n* **Reckless**: You have little regard for your own safety or best interests.\n* **Soft**: You lose your edge; you become sentimental, passive, gentle.\n* **Unstable**: Your emotional state is volatile. You can instantly rage, or fall into despair, act impulsively, or freeze up.\n* **Vicious**: You seek out opportunities to hurt people, even for no good reason."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-1-progress-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Progress clocks",
      "slug": "5-1-progress-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.1",
        "heading": "Progress clocks",
        "lineStart": 247,
        "lineEnd": 263
      },
      "section": "5.1",
      "rulesText": "A **progress clock** is a circle divided into segments (see examples at right). Draw a progress clock when you need to track ongoing effort against an obstacle or the approach of impending trouble.\n\n@TODO add clock illustration\n\n*Sneaking into the constables watch tower? Make a clock to track the alert level of the patrolling guards. When the PCs suffer consequences from partial successes or missed rolls, fill in segments on the clock until the alarm is raised.*\n\nGenerally, the more complex the problem, the more segments in the progress clock.\n\nA complex obstacle is a 4-segment clock. A more complicated obstacle is a 6-clock. A daunting obstacle is an 8-segment clock.\n\nWhen you create a clock, make it about the **obstacle,** not the method. The clocks for an infiltration should be \"Interior Patrols\" and \"The Tower,\" not \"Sneak Past the Guards\" or \"Climb the Tower.\" The patrols and the tower are the obstacles - the PCs can attempt to overcome them in a variety of ways.\n\nComplex enemy threats can be broken into several \"layers,\" each with its own progress clock. For example, the dockside gangs' HQ might have a \"Perimeter Security\" clock, an \"Interior Guards\" clock, and a \"Office Security\" clock. The crew would have to make their way through all three layers to reach the gang boss' personal safe and valuables within.\n\nRemember that a clock tracks progress. It reflects the fictional situation, so the group can gauge how they're doing. A clock is like a speedometer in a car. It *shows* the speed of the vehicle—it doesn't determine the speed."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-2-simple-obstacles",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Simple Obstacles",
      "slug": "5-2-simple-obstacles",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.2",
        "heading": "Simple Obstacles",
        "lineStart": 265,
        "lineEnd": 269
      },
      "section": "5.2",
      "rulesText": "Not every situation and obstacle requires a clock. Use clocks when a situation is complex or layered and you need to track something over time—otherwise, resolve the result of an action with a single roll.\n\nExamples of progress clocks follow."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-3-danger-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Danger Clocks",
      "slug": "5-3-danger-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.3",
        "heading": "Danger Clocks",
        "lineStart": 271,
        "lineEnd": 273
      },
      "section": "5.3",
      "rulesText": "The GM can use a clock to represent a progressive danger, like suspicion growing during a seduction, the proximity of pursuers in a chase, or the alert level of guards on patrol. In this case, when a complication occurs, the GM ticks one, two, or three segments on the clock, depending on the consequence level. When the clock is full, the danger comes to fruition—the guards hunt down the intruders, activate an alarm, release the hounds, etc."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-4-racing-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Racing Clocks",
      "slug": "5-4-racing-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.4",
        "heading": "Racing Clocks",
        "lineStart": 275,
        "lineEnd": 279
      },
      "section": "5.4",
      "rulesText": "Create two opposed clocks to represent a race. The PCs might have a progress clock called \"Escape\" while the constables have a clock called \"Cornered.\" If the PCs finish their clock before the constables fill theirs, they get away. Otherwise, they're cornered and can't flee. If both complete at the same time, the PCs escape to their lair, but the hunting officers are outside!\n\nYou can also use racing clocks for an environmental hazard. Maybe the PCs are trying to complete the \"Search\" clock to find the lockbox on the sinking ship before the GM fills the \"Sunk\" clock and the vessel goes down."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-5-linked-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Linked Clocks",
      "slug": "5-5-linked-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.5",
        "heading": "Linked Clocks",
        "lineStart": 281,
        "lineEnd": 283
      },
      "section": "5.5",
      "rulesText": "You can make a clock that unlocks another clock once it's filled. For example, the GM might make a linked clock called \"Trapped\" after an \"Alert\" clock fills up. When you fight a veteran warrior, she might have a clock for her \"Defense\" and then a linked clock for \"Vulnerable.\" Once you overcome the \"Defense\" clock, then you can attempt to overcome the \"Vulnerable\" clock and defeat her.  You might affect the \"Defense\" clock with violence in a knife-fight, or you lower her defense with deception if you have the opportunity. As always, the method of action is up to the players and the details of the fiction at hand."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-6-mission-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Mission Clocks",
      "slug": "5-6-mission-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.6",
        "heading": "Mission Clocks",
        "lineStart": 285,
        "lineEnd": 287
      },
      "section": "5.6",
      "rulesText": "The GM can make a clock for a time-sensitive mission, to represent the window of opportunity you have to complete it. If the countdown runs out, the mission is scrubbed or changes—the target escapes, the household wakes up for the day, etc."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-7-tug-of-war-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Tug-of-war Clocks",
      "slug": "5-7-tug-of-war-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.7",
        "heading": "Tug-of-war Clocks",
        "lineStart": 289,
        "lineEnd": 291
      },
      "section": "5.7",
      "rulesText": "You can make a clock that can be filled *and* emptied by events, to represent a back-and-forth situation. You might make a \"Revolution!\" clock that indicates when the refugees start to riot over poor treatment. Some events will tick the clock up and some will tick it down. Once it fills, the revolution begins. A tug-of-war clock is also perfect for an ongoing turf war between two crews or factions."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-8-long-term-project",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Long-term Project",
      "slug": "5-8-long-term-project",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.8",
        "heading": "Long-term Project",
        "lineStart": 293,
        "lineEnd": 297
      },
      "section": "5.8",
      "rulesText": "Some projects will take a long time. A basic long-term project (like tinkering up a new feature for a device) is eight segments. Truly long-term projects (like creating a new designer drug) can be two, three, or even four clocks, representing all the phases of development, testing, and final completion. Add or subtract clocks depending on the details of the situation and complexity of the project.\n\nA long-term project is a good catch-all for dealing with any unusual player goal, including things that circumvent or change elements of the mechanics or the setting."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/5-9-faction-clocks",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Faction Clocks",
      "slug": "5-9-faction-clocks",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Progress clocks",
        "section": "5.9",
        "heading": "Faction Clocks",
        "lineStart": 299,
        "lineEnd": 303
      },
      "section": "5.9",
      "rulesText": "Each faction has a long-term goal. When the PCs have **downtime**, the GM ticks forward the faction clocks that they're interested in. In this way, the world around the PCs is dynamic and things happen that they're not directly connected to, changing the overall situation in the city and creating new opportunities and challenges.\n\nThe PCs may also directly affect NPC faction clocks, based on the missions and scores they pull off. Discuss known faction projects that they might aid or interfere with, and also consider how a PC operation might affect the NPC clocks, whether the players intended it or not."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-1-action-roll",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Action Roll",
      "slug": "6-1-action-roll",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.1",
        "heading": "Action Roll",
        "lineStart": 305,
        "lineEnd": 318
      },
      "section": "6.1",
      "rulesText": "When a player character does something challenging, we make an **action roll** to see how it turns out. An action is challenging if there's an obstacle to the PC's goal that's dangerous or troublesome in some way. We don't make an action roll unless the PC is put to the test. If their action is something that we'd expect them to simply accomplish, then we don't make an action roll.\n\n*Each game group will have their own ideas about what \"challenging\" means. This is good! It's something that establishes the tone and style of your Blades series.*\n\nTo make an action roll, we go through six steps. In play, they flow together somewhat, but let's break each one down here for clarity.\n\n* The player states their **goal** for the action.\n* The player chooses the **action rating**.\n* The GM sets the **position** for the roll.\n* The GM sets the **effect level** for the action.\n* Add **bonus dice**.\n* The player rolls the dice and we judge the result."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-10-action-roll",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "ACTION ROLL",
      "slug": "6-10-action-roll",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.10",
        "heading": "ACTION ROLL",
        "lineStart": 390,
        "lineEnd": 396
      },
      "section": "6.10",
      "rulesText": "&#x23E3; &nbsp; **1d** for each **Action** rating dot.\n\n&#x2295; &nbsp; **+1d** if you have **Assistance**.\n\n&#x2295; &nbsp; **+1d** if you **Push** yourself -or- you accept a **Devil's Bargain**."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-11-controlled",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "CONTROLLED",
      "slug": "6-11-controlled",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.11",
        "heading": "CONTROLLED",
        "lineStart": 398,
        "lineEnd": 409
      },
      "section": "6.11",
      "rulesText": "*You act on your terms. You exploit a dominant advantage.*\n\n* **Critical**: You do it with **increased effect**.\n* **6**: You do it.\n* **4/5**: You hesitate.\n    - Withdraw and try a different approach, or\n    - else do it with a minor consequence: a **minor complication** occurs, you have **reduced effect**, you suffer **lesser harm**, you end up in a **risky** position.\n* **1-3**: You falter.\n    - Press on by seizing a **risky** opportunity, or\n    - withdraw and try a different approach."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-12-risky",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "RISKY",
      "slug": "6-12-risky",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.12",
        "heading": "RISKY",
        "lineStart": 411,
        "lineEnd": 418
      },
      "section": "6.12",
      "rulesText": "*You go head to head. You act under fire. You take a chance.*\n\n* **Critical**: You do it with **increased effect**.\n* **6**: You do it.\n* **4/5**: You do it, but there's a consequence: you suffer **harm**, a **complication** occurs, you have **reduced effect**, you end up in a **desperate** position.\n* **1-3**: Things go badly. You suffer **harm**, a **complication** occurs, you end up in a **desperate** position, you **lose this opportunity**."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-13-desperate",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "DESPERATE",
      "slug": "6-13-desperate",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.13",
        "heading": "DESPERATE",
        "lineStart": 420,
        "lineEnd": 429
      },
      "section": "6.13",
      "rulesText": "*You overreach your capabilities. You're in serious trouble.*\n\n* **Critical**: You do it with **increased effect**.\n* **6**: You do it.\n* **4/5**: You do it, but there's a consequence: you suffer **severe harm**, a **serious complication** occurs, you have **reduced effect**.\n* **1-3**: It's the worst outcome. You suffer **severe harm**, a **serious complication** occurs, you **lose this opportunity** for action."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-14-double-duty-rolls",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Double-duty Rolls",
      "slug": "6-14-double-duty-rolls",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.14",
        "heading": "Double-duty Rolls",
        "lineStart": 432,
        "lineEnd": 434
      },
      "section": "6.14",
      "rulesText": "Since NPCs don't roll for their actions, an action roll does double-duty: **it resolves the action of the PC as well as any NPCs that are involved**. The single roll tells us how those actions interact and which consequences result. On a **6**, the PC wins and has their effect. On a **4/5**, it's a mix—both the PC and the NPC have their effect. On a **1-3**, the NPC wins and has their effect as a consequence on the PC."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-2-1-the-player-states-their-goal",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "1. The Player States Their Goal",
      "slug": "6-2-1-the-player-states-their-goal",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.2",
        "heading": "1. The Player States Their Goal",
        "lineStart": 320,
        "lineEnd": 324
      },
      "section": "6.2",
      "rulesText": "Your goal is the concrete outcome your character will achieve when they overcome the obstacle at hand. Usually the character's goal is pretty obvious in context, but it's the GM's job to ask and clarify the goal when necessary.\n\n*\"You're punching him in the face, right? Okay... what do want to get out of this? Do you want to take him out, or just rough him up so he'll do what you want?\"*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-3-2-the-player-chooses-the-action-rating",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "2. The Player Chooses the Action Rating",
      "slug": "6-3-2-the-player-chooses-the-action-rating",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.3",
        "heading": "2. The Player Chooses the Action Rating",
        "lineStart": 326,
        "lineEnd": 328
      },
      "section": "6.3",
      "rulesText": "The player chooses which **action rating** to roll, following from what their character is doing on-screen. If you want to roll your **Skirmish** action, then get in a fight. If you want to roll your **Command** action, then order someone around. You can't roll a given action rating unless your character is presently performing that action in the fiction."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-4-3-the-gm-sets-the-position",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "3. The GM Sets the Position",
      "slug": "6-4-3-the-gm-sets-the-position",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.4",
        "heading": "3. The GM Sets the Position",
        "lineStart": 330,
        "lineEnd": 334
      },
      "section": "6.4",
      "rulesText": "Once the player chooses their action, the GM sets the **position** for the roll. The position represents how dangerous or troublesome the action might be. There are three positions: **controlled**, **risky**, and **desperate**. To choose a position, the GM looks at the profiles for the positions below and picks one that most closely matches the situation at hand.\n\n**By default, an action roll is risky.** You wouldn't be rolling if there was no risk involved. If the situation seems more dangerous, make it desperate. If it seems less dangerous, make it controlled."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-5-4-the-gm-sets-the-effect-level",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "4. The GM Sets the Effect Level",
      "slug": "6-5-4-the-gm-sets-the-effect-level",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.5",
        "heading": "4. The GM Sets the Effect Level",
        "lineStart": 336,
        "lineEnd": 340
      },
      "section": "6.5",
      "rulesText": "The GM assesses the likely **effect level** of this action, given the factors of the situation. Essentially, the effect level tells us \"how much\" this action can accomplish: will it have **limited**, **standard**, or **great** effect?\n\n*The GM's choices for effect level and position can be strongly influenced by the player's choice of action rating. If a player wants to try to make a new friend by **Wrecking** something—well... maybe that's possible, but the GM wouldn't be crazy to say it's a desperate roll and probably limited effect. Seems like **Consorting** would be a lot better for that. The players are always free to choose the action they perform, but that doesn't mean all actions should be equally risky or potent.*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-6-5-add-bonus-dice",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "5. Add Bonus Dice",
      "slug": "6-6-5-add-bonus-dice",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.6",
        "heading": "5. Add Bonus Dice",
        "lineStart": 342,
        "lineEnd": 348
      },
      "section": "6.6",
      "rulesText": "You can normally get two bonus dice for your action roll (some special abilities might give you additional bonus dice).\n\nFor one bonus die, you can get **assistance** from a teammate. They take 1 stress, say how they help you, and give you +1d.\n\nFor another bonus die, you can either **push yourself** (take 2 stress) or you can accept a **Devil's Bargain** (you can't get dice for both, it's one or the other)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-7-the-devils-bargain",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "The Devil's Bargain",
      "slug": "6-7-the-devils-bargain",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.7",
        "heading": "The Devil's Bargain",
        "lineStart": 350,
        "lineEnd": 366
      },
      "section": "6.7",
      "rulesText": "PCs in *Blades* are reckless scoundrels addicted to destructive vices—they don't always act in their own best interests. To reflect this, the GM or any other player can offer you a bonus die if you accept a Devil's Bargain. Common Devil's Bargains include:\n\n* Collateral damage, unintended harm.\n* Sacrifice **coin** or an item.\n* Betray a friend or loved one.\n* Offend or anger a faction.\n* Start and/or tick a troublesome clock.\n* Add **heat** to the crew from evidence or witnesses.\n* Suffer harm.\n\nThe Devil's Bargain occurs regardless of the outcome of the roll. You make the deal, pay the price, and get the bonus die.\n\nThe Devil's Bargain is always a free choice. If you don't like one, just reject it (or suggest how to alter it so you might consider taking it). You can always just push yourself for that bonus die instead.\n\nIf it's ever needed, the GM has final say over which Devil's Bargains are valid."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-8-6-roll-the-dice-and-judge-the-result",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "6. Roll the Dice and Judge the Result",
      "slug": "6-8-6-roll-the-dice-and-judge-the-result",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.8",
        "heading": "6. Roll the Dice and Judge the Result",
        "lineStart": 368,
        "lineEnd": 378
      },
      "section": "6.8",
      "rulesText": "Once the goal, action rating, position, and effect have been established, add any bonus dice and roll the dice pool to determine the outcome. (See the sets of possible outcomes, by position, in the next sections.)\n\nThe action roll does a lot of work for you. It tells you how well the character performs as well as how serious the consequences are for them. They might succeed at their action without any consequences (on a **6**), or they might succeed but suffer consequences (on a **4/5**), or it might just all go wrong (on a **1-3**).\n\nOn a **1-3**, it's up to the GM to decide if the PC's action has any effect or not, or if it even happens at all. Usually, the action just fails completely, but in some circumstances, it might make sense or be more interesting for the action to have some effect even on a **1-3** result.\n\nEach **4/5** and **1-3** outcome lists suggested **consequences** for the character. The worse your position, the worse the consequences are. The GM can inflict one or more of these consequences, depending on the circumstances of the action roll. PCs have the ability to avoid or reduce the severity of consequences that they suffer by **resisting** them.\n\nWhen you narrate the action after the roll, the GM and player collaborate together to say what happens on-screen. *Tell us how you vault across to the other rooftop. Tell us what you say to the Inspector to convince her. The GM will tell us how she reacts. When you face the Red Sash duelist, what's your fighting style like? Etc.*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/6-9-action-roll-summary",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Action Roll Summary",
      "slug": "6-9-action-roll-summary",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Action Roll",
        "section": "6.9",
        "heading": "Action Roll Summary",
        "lineStart": 380,
        "lineEnd": 388
      },
      "section": "6.9",
      "rulesText": "* A player or GM calls for a roll. Make an **action roll** when the character performs a dangerous or troublesome action.\n* The player chooses the **action rating** to roll. Choose the action that matches what the character is doing in the fiction.\n* The GM establishes the **position** and **effect level** of the action. The choice of position and effect is influenced strongly by the player's choice of action.\n* Add up to two bonus dice. 1) **Assistance** from a teammate. 2) **Push yourself** (take 2 stress) or accept a **Devil's Bargain**.\n* Roll the dice pool and judge the outcome. The players and GM narrate the action together. The GM has final say over what happens and inflicts consequences as called for by the position and the result of the roll."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-1-effect",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Effect",
      "slug": "7-1-effect",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.1",
        "heading": "Effect",
        "lineStart": 436,
        "lineEnd": 444
      },
      "section": "7.1",
      "rulesText": "In *Blades in the Dark*, you achieve goals by taking actions and facing consequences. But how many actions does it take to achieve a particular goal? That depends on the **effect level** of your actions. The GM judges the effect level using the profiles below. Which one best matches the action at hand -- **great**, **standard**, or **limited**? Each effect level indicates the questions that should be answered for that effect, as well as how many segments to tick if you're using a **progress clock**.",
      "relatedTables": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/tables/7-1-effect"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-2-assessing-factors",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Assessing Factors",
      "slug": "7-2-assessing-factors",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.2",
        "heading": "Assessing Factors",
        "lineStart": 446,
        "lineEnd": 448
      },
      "section": "7.2",
      "rulesText": "To assess effect level, first start with your gut feeling, given this situation. Then, if needed, assess three factors that may modify the effect level: **potency**, **scale**, and **quality**. If the PC has an advantage in a given factor, consider a higher effect level. If they have a disadvantage, consider a reduced effect level."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-3-potency",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Potency",
      "slug": "7-3-potency",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.3",
        "heading": "Potency",
        "lineStart": 450,
        "lineEnd": 452
      },
      "section": "7.3",
      "rulesText": "The potency factor considers particular weaknesses, taking extra time or a bigger risk, or the influence of arcane powers. An infiltrator is more potent if all the lights are extinguished and they move about in the dark."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-4-quality-tier",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Quality/Tier",
      "slug": "7-4-quality-tier",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.4",
        "heading": "Quality/Tier",
        "lineStart": 454,
        "lineEnd": 458
      },
      "section": "7.4",
      "rulesText": "Quality represents the effectiveness of tools, weapons, or other resources, usually summarized by Tier. **Fine items** count as +1 bonus in quality, stacking with Tier.\n\n*Thorn is picking the lock to a safehouse run of a gang renowned for Occult dealings. Her crew is Tier I and she has fine lock picks - so she's effectively Tier II. The Occult gang is Tier III. Thorn is outclassed in quality, so her effect will be limited on the lock.*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-5-scale",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Scale",
      "slug": "7-5-scale",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.5",
        "heading": "Scale",
        "lineStart": 460,
        "lineEnd": 470
      },
      "section": "7.5",
      "rulesText": "Scale represents the number of opponents, size of an area covered, scope of influence, etc. Larger scale can be an advantage or disadvantage depending on the situation. In battle, more people are better. When infiltrating, more people are a hindrance.\n\nWhen considering factors, effect level might be reduced below limited, resulting in **zero effect**—or increased beyond great, resulting in an **extreme effect**.\n\nIf a PC special ability gives \"+1 effect,\" it comes into play *after* the GM has assessed the effect level. For example, if you ended up with zero effect, the +1 effect bonus from your Cutter's **Bodyguard** ability would bump them up to limited effect.\n\nAlso, remember that a PC can **push themselves** (take 2 stress) to get +1 effect on their action.\n\nEvery factor won't always apply to every situation. You don't have to do an exact accounting every time, either. Use the factors to help you make a stronger judgment call—don't feel beholden to them."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-6-trading-position-for-effect",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Trading Position for Effect",
      "slug": "7-6-trading-position-for-effect",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.6",
        "heading": "Trading Position for Effect",
        "lineStart": 472,
        "lineEnd": 484
      },
      "section": "7.6",
      "rulesText": "After factors are considered and the GM has announced the effect level, a player might want to trade position for effect, or vice versa. For instance, if they're going to make a risky roll with standard effect (the most common scenario, generally), they might instead want to push their luck and make a desperate roll but with great effect.\n\nThis kind of trade-off isn't included in the effect factors because it's not an element the GM should assess when setting the effect level. Once the level is set, though, you can always offer the trade-off to the player if it makes sense in the situation.\n\n*\"I Prowl across the courtyard and vault over the wall, hiding in the shadows by the canal dock and gondola.\"*\n\n*\"I don't think you can make it across in one quick dash. The scale of the courtyard is a factor here, so your effect will be limited. Let's say you can get halfway across with this action, then you'll have to Prowl through the other half of the space (and the rest of the guards there) to reach the other side.\"*\n\n*\"I didn't realize it was that far. Hmmm. Okay, what if I just go as fast as I can. Can I get all the way across if I make a desperate roll?\"*\n\n*\"Yep, sounds good to me!\"*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/7-7-consequences",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Consequences",
      "slug": "7-7-consequences",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Effect",
        "section": "7.7",
        "heading": "Consequences",
        "lineStart": 486,
        "lineEnd": 488
      },
      "section": "7.7",
      "rulesText": "When a PC suffers an effect from an enemy or a dangerous situation, it's called a **consequence**. Consequences are the companion to effects. PCs have effect on the world around them and they suffer consequences in return from the risks they face."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/8-1-setting-position-effect",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Setting Position & Effect",
      "slug": "8-1-setting-position-effect",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Setting Position & Effect",
        "section": "8.1",
        "heading": "Setting Position & Effect",
        "lineStart": 490,
        "lineEnd": 501
      },
      "section": "8.1",
      "rulesText": "The GM sets position and effect for an action roll at the same time, after the player says what they're doing and chooses their action. Usually, **Risky / Standard** is the default combination, modified by the action being used, the strength of the opposition, and the effect factors.\n\nThe ability to set position and effect as independent variables gives you nine combinations to choose from, to help you convey a wide array of fictional circumstances.\n\n*For example, if a character is facing off alone against a small enemy gang, the situation might be:*\n\n* *She fights the gang straight up, rushing into their midst, hacking away in a wild ***Skirmish***. In this case, being threatened by the larger force lowers her position to indicate greater risk, and the scale of the gang reduces her effect (Desperate / Limited).*\n* *She fights the gang from a choke-point, like a narrow alleyway where their numbers can't overwhelm her at once. She's not threatened by several at once, so her risk is similar to a one-on-one fight, but there's still a lot of enemies to deal with, so her effect is reduced (Risky / Limited).*\n* *She doesn't fight the gang, instead trying to maneuver her way past them and escape. She's still under threat from many enemy attacks, so her position is worse, but if the ground is open and the gang can't easily corral her, then her effect for escaping isn't reduced (Desperate / Standard). If she had some immediate means of escape (like leaping onto a speeding carriage), then her effect might even be increased (Desperate / Great).*\n* *The gang isn't aware of her yet—she's set up in a sniper position on a nearby roof. She takes a shot against one of them. Their greater numbers aren't a factor, so her effect isn't reduced, and she's not immediately in any danger (Controlled / Great). Maybe instead she wants to fire off a salvo of suppressing fire against the whole gang, in which case their scale applies (Controlled / Limited). If the gang is on guard for potential trouble, her position is more dangerous (Risky / Great). If the gang is alerted to a sniper, then the effect may be reduced further, as they scatter and take cover (Risky / Limited). If the gang is able to muster covering fire while they fall back to a safe position, then things are even worse for our scoundrel (Desperate / Limited).*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-1-consequences-and-harm",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Consequences and Harm",
      "slug": "9-1-consequences-and-harm",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.1",
        "heading": "Consequences and Harm",
        "lineStart": 503,
        "lineEnd": 507
      },
      "section": "9.1",
      "rulesText": "Enemy actions, bad circumstances, or the outcome of a roll can inflict **consequences** on a PC. There are five types (at right).\n\nA given circumstance might result in one or more consequences, depending on the situation. The GM determines the consequences, following from the fiction and the style and tone established by the game group."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-2-reduced-effect",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Reduced Effect",
      "slug": "9-2-reduced-effect",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.2",
        "heading": "Reduced Effect",
        "lineStart": 509,
        "lineEnd": 511
      },
      "section": "9.2",
      "rulesText": "This consequence represents impaired performance. The PC's action isn't as effective as they'd anticipated. You hit him, but it's only a flesh wound. She accepts the forged invitation, but she'll keep her eye on you throughout the night. You're able to scale the wall, but it's slow going—you're only halfway up. This consequence essentially reduces the effect level of the PC's action by one after all other factors are accounted for."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-3-complication",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Complication",
      "slug": "9-3-complication",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.3",
        "heading": "Complication",
        "lineStart": 513,
        "lineEnd": 521
      },
      "section": "9.3",
      "rulesText": "This consequence represents trouble, mounting danger, or a new threat. The GM might introduce an immediate problem that results from the action right now: the room catches fire, you're disarmed, the crew takes +1 **heat** from evidence or witnesses, you lose status with a faction, the target evades you and now it's a chase, reinforcements arrive, etc.\n\nOr the GM might tick a clock for the complication, instead. Maybe there's a clock for the alert level of the guards at the manor. Or maybe the GM creates a new clock for the suspicion of the noble guests at the masquerade party and ticks it. Fill one tick on a clock for a minor complication or two ticks for a standard complication.\n\nA **serious complication** is more severe: reinforcements surround and trap you, the room catches fire and falling ceiling beams block the door, your weapon is broken, the crew suffers +2 **heat**, your target escapes out of sight, etc. Fill three ticks on a clock for a serious complication.\n\n**Don't inflict a complication that negates a successful roll.** If a PC tries to corner an enemy and gets a **4/5**, don't say that the enemy escapes. The player's roll succeeded, so the enemy is cornered... maybe the PC has to wrestle them into position and during the scuffle the enemy grabs their gun."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-4-lost-opportunity",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Lost Opportunity",
      "slug": "9-4-lost-opportunity",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.4",
        "heading": "Lost Opportunity",
        "lineStart": 523,
        "lineEnd": 525
      },
      "section": "9.4",
      "rulesText": "This consequence represents shifting circumstance. You had an opportunity to achieve your goal with this action, but it slips away. To try again, you need a new approach—usually a new form of action or a change in circumstances. Maybe you tried to **Skirmish** with the noble to trap her on the balcony, but she evades your maneuver and leaps out of reach. If you want to trap her now you'll have to try another way—maybe by **Swaying** her with your roguish charm."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-5-worse-position",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Worse Position",
      "slug": "9-5-worse-position",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.5",
        "heading": "Worse Position",
        "lineStart": 527,
        "lineEnd": 529
      },
      "section": "9.5",
      "rulesText": "This consequence represents losing control of the situation—the action carries you into a more dangerous position. Perhaps you make the leap across to the next rooftop, only to end up dangling by your fingertips. You haven't failed, but you haven't succeeded yet, either. You can try again, re-rolling at the new, worse position. This is a good consequence to choose to show escalating action. A situation might go from controlled, to risky, to desperate as the action plays out and the PC gets deeper and deeper in trouble."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-6-harm",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Harm",
      "slug": "9-6-harm",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.6",
        "heading": "Harm",
        "lineStart": 531,
        "lineEnd": 539
      },
      "section": "9.6",
      "rulesText": "This consequence represents a long-lasting debility (or death). When you suffer harm, record the specific injury on your character sheet equal to the level of harm you suffer. If you suffer **lesser harm**, record it in the bottom row. If you suffer **moderate harm**, write it in the middle row. If you suffer **severe harm**, record it in the top row. See examples of harm and the harm tracker, below.\n\nYour character suffers the penalty indicated at the end of the row if any or all harm recorded in that row applies to the situation at hand. So, if you have \"Drained\" and \"Battered\" harm in the bottom row, you'll suffer reduced effect when you try to run away from the constables. When you're impaired by harm in the top row (severe harm, level 3), your character is incapacitated and can't do anything unless you have help from someone else or **push yourself** to perform the action.\n\nIf you need to mark a harm level, but the row is already filled, the harm moves up to the next row above. So, if you suffered standard harm (level 2) but had no empty spaces in the second row, you'd have to record severe harm (level 3), instead. If you run out of spaces on the top row and need to mark harm there, your character suffers a **catastrophic, permanent consequence** (loss of a limb, sudden death, etc., depending on the circumstances)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-7-harm",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "HARM",
      "slug": "9-7-harm",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.7",
        "heading": "HARM",
        "lineStart": 541,
        "lineEnd": 551
      },
      "section": "9.7",
      "rulesText": "*This character has three harm: a \"Shattered Right Leg\" (level 3) plus \"Drained\" and \"Battered\" (level 1). If they suffer another level 1 harm, it will move up to level 2. If they suffer another level 3 harm, it will move up to level 4: Fatal.*",
      "relatedTables": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/tables/9-7-harm"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/rules/9-8-harm-examples",
      "@type": "Rule",
      "name": "Harm examples",
      "slug": "9-8-harm-examples",
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd"
      },
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "Consequences and Harm",
        "section": "9.8",
        "heading": "Harm examples",
        "lineStart": 553,
        "lineEnd": 563
      },
      "section": "9.8",
      "rulesText": "**Fatal (4)**: *Electrocuted, Drowned, Stabbed in the Heart.*\n\n**Severe (3)**: *Impaled, Broken Leg, Shot in Chest, Badly Burned, Terrified.*\n\n**Moderate (2)**: *Exhausted, Deep Cut to Arm, Concussion, Panicked, Seduced.*\n\n**Lesser (1)**: *Battered, Drained, Distracted, Scared, Confused.*\n\nHarm like \"Drained\" or \"Exhausted\" can be a good fallback consequence if there's nothing else threatening a PC (like when they spend all night **Studying** those old books, looking for any clues to their enemy's weaknesses before he strikes)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/fitd-system-json/objects/sources/blades-in-the-dark-srd",
      "@type": "Source",
      "name": "Blades in the Dark SRD",
      "slug": "blades-in-the-dark-srd",
      "author": "John Harper",
      "publisher": "One Seven Design",
      "sourceVersion": "amazingrando/blades-in-the-dark-srd-content@ac2747ff (2022-04-08)",
      "license": "CC-BY-3.0",
